{
  "families": [
    {
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 8, "high": 22, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -1, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.35,
      "why": "Baseline fully pipelined family: 1 result/cycle, highest Fmax, so it easily satisfies throughput >= 32 Msps and the 0.44 us batch deadline at typical clocks. It gives the reference area/accuracy trade-off against which pipelined_m must improve. Ranges cover the accuracy boundary (W=12..20, N=8..22) and small guard-bit variations that affect area and error."
    },
    {
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 20, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 8, "high": 22, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -1, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "m", "low": 2, "high": 8, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.65,
      "why": "Registering every m stages cuts flip-flop count relative to the fully pipelined family, which directly targets the min luts_plus_ffs objective. The m parameter trades area against Fmax and latency; larger m reduces FFs but may lower Fmax. We give it the larger share because it is the most likely source of Pareto-optimal area points. Ranges match pipelined for accuracy parameters and add m=2..8 to map the area/Fmax trade-off while keeping throughput and batch latency feasible."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "Only pipelined and pipelined_m are allowed. Both produce 1 result/cycle, so the 32 Msps throughput and 0.44 us batch deadline are met as long as Fmax stays above roughly 73 MHz (for 32 results in 0.44 us) and the pipeline latency plus 31 cycles fits in 0.44 us; at expected Fmax this is not restrictive. Accuracy requires max_abs_err <= 2^-12, so data_width must be at least about 14 and n_iter must be sufficient; the ranges include lower values to let the search locate the feasibility boundary. Area is dominated by data_width x n_iter and pipeline registers, so pipelined_m with larger m is the main lever for the min luts_plus_ffs objective. The budget split favours pipelined_m while keeping a solid baseline of fully pipelined designs."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "next_plan": null,
  "rationale": "Final round (round cap reached). The front is feasible and well-formed, so `infeasible` does not apply: all three constraints are satisfiable — throughput_msps >= 32 has 0% violations (best 282, and even the cheapest front point delivers 164 MSPS), max_abs_err <= 2^-12 is met by every point on the front (best 2.32e-05), and sys_p99_batch_us <= 0.44 has 23% violations overall but is satisfied by the whole Pareto front (best 0.147). The front already spans the meaningful objective ranges: luts_plus_ffs 1334..2473 against the HV reference 4000, and accuracy_bits 12.3..15.4 against the reference 12, covering both objectives well past their reference points. The selection rule (min luts_plus_ffs) picks pipelined_m [data_width=18 n_iter=14 angle_guard=0 frac_guard=1 rounding=trunc m=2] at luts_plus_ffs=1334, accuracy_bits=12.3, max_abs_err=0.000205 <= 2^-12, throughput 164 MSPS >= 32, sys_p99_batch 0.147 <= 0.44 — feasible on all constraints. This cheapest point sits comfortably inside each constraint margin (accuracy ~8% below the limit, batch latency ~3x below the limit), so it is not a borderline L1-only pass likely to be culled at L2; the L2 re-selection has margin to fall back on the neighbouring front points (1377, 1415). Stopping, with the front left fitted for the spec's selection rule."}
